discussion:
The theorem settles the question posed in arXiv:2610.10559 by disproving that $c(n) = \lceil 2n/3 - 1 \rceil$ holds for all $n \ge 3$.

While the contradiction in `not_forall_lly_positive_edge_connectivity_threshold_eq_ceil` relies on $n = 3$, the failure of the formula is not merely a small, degenerate artifact. Exact computations of $c(n)$ for $n \le 6$ (computed via Theorem 2.2 of arXiv:2610.10559 and minimum-cost flow) yield:

- $c(3) = 0$, whereas $\lceil 2 \cdot 3 / 3 - 1 \rceil = 1$;
- $c(4) = 2$, whereas $\lceil 2 \cdot 4 / 3 - 1 \rceil = 2$;
- $c(5) = 2$, whereas $\lceil 2 \cdot 5 / 3 - 1 \rceil = 3$;
- $c(6) = 3$, whereas $\lceil 2 \cdot 6 / 3 - 1 \rceil = 3$.

As established by `lly_positive_edge_connectivity_threshold_five`, the threshold also fails at $n = 5$, where $c(5) = 2 < 3$.

The positive result `lly_curvature_pos_of_edge_connectivity_ge` guarantees that an edge-connectivity of at least $2n/3 - 1$ ensures strictly positive Lin–Lu–Yau curvature on every edge. Theorem 1.4 of arXiv:2610.10559 provides sharpness by constructing graphs with edge-connectivity $2n/3 - 2$ having an edge of negative curvature, but only when $2n/3$ is an even integer at least $6$. For general values of $n$—in particular when $2n/3$ is not an even integer—the exact minimum edge-connectivity threshold $c(n)$ guaranteeing everywhere positive Lin–Lu–Yau curvature remains open.
